================================================================================================================
V1 -- SYMPY: the MI relation and the QUMOND prescription are the same map
================================================================================================================
  MI defines   y = x mu(x)   (y = g_tot/a0, x = a/a0),  boost = x/y
  QUMOND defines nu(y) = 1/mu(x(y)) on the SAME implicit relation
  1/mu(x) - x/y  evaluated at y = x mu(x)  =  0
  [PASS] V1 MI's a(g_N) map and QUMOND's nu-map are the SAME function on the same implicit relation, for ANY mu -- the EFE of modified inertia IS the algebraic (QUMOND) EFE, not a different one
         (the identity 1/mu(x) = x/(x mu(x)) holds identically (sympy, abstract mu); a concrete kernel is checked next)
  concrete (a0-line kernel): x = [ 0.3  1.   3.  10. ]  y = x mu(x) = [0.0831 0.618  2.5414 9.5125]
    x/y from the MI relation = [3.610317 1.618034 1.18046  1.051249]
    nu_line(y)             = [3.610317 1.618034 1.18046  1.051249]
  [PASS] V1b a concrete kernel reproduces the identity numerically (a0-line: nu = sqrt(1+1/y))
         (max rel diff 4.44e-16 over x = 0.3-10)

